<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout xml:id="pp-1">
    <title>Portfolio Problem 1 - Play Conjecture Prove</title>

    <p>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Option 1: Chessboard covering problems</title>

                <p>
                    Pick a conjecture from class about chess- boards, or another
                    chessboard mystery you have been wondering about.
                    Either way, make a conjecture and prove it!
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <title>Option 2: The game SET</title>

                <p>
                    Pick a conjecture about the game SET from class, or
                    something you are curious about, and prove it!
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <title>Option 3: Eeeny Meeny Miny Moe </title>

                <p>
                    Remember the childhood game for deciding who is <q>it</q>-
                    eeny meeny miny moe?
                    How could you <q>win</q> this game?
                    This could go in a number of different directions.
                    Different number of people?
                    Different rhyme?
                    Do you want to be the last person pointed at or not?
                    What if you do <q>elimination</q> and go until one only one
                    person is left.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <title>Option 4: The pancake problem</title>

                <p>
                    The chef in our place is sloppy and when he prepares a stack
                    of pancakes they come out all di!erent sizes.
                    Therefore, when I deliver them to the customer, on the way
                    to the table I rearrange them (so the smallest winds up on
                    top and so on, down to the largest is on the bottom) by
                    grabbing several from the top and flipping them over,
                    repeating this (varying the number I flip) as many times as
                    necessary.
                    If there are n pancakes, what is the maximum number of flips
                    (in terms of n) that I will ever have to use to rearrange
                    them?
                    Note: For this problem, you could do a proof for the maximum
                    number of flips for 3 pancakes.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <title>Option 5: Crop Circles</title>

                <p>
                    The aliens came last night to make crop circles and they
                    blew around some seeds on your field.
                    Any acre that has multiple seed types is ruined.
                    You know the following facts.
                    How many acres are unruined?
                    You should state your answer as a theorem and prove it.
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                There are 68 total acres
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                60 acres have tomato, corn, or carrot seeds
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                37 acres have tomato seeds
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                36 acres have corn seeds
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                40 acres have carrot seeds
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                21 acres have tomato and carrot seeds
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                25 acres have carrot and corn seeds
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                24 acres have tomato and corn seeds
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                17 acres have tomato, corn, and carrot seeds
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <title>Option 6: <m>n</m>-factors</title>

                <p>
                    For a natural number <m>n</m> we'll call an integer <m>a</m>
                    an <term><m>n</m>-factor</term> if
                    <m>a \not\equiv 0 \pmod{n}</m> and there exists an integer
                    <m>b</m> such that <m>b \not\equiv 0 \pmod{n}</m> and
                    <m>ab \equiv 0 \pmod{n}</m>.
                    Find 3 examples of <m>n</m>-factors using 3 different values
                    of <m>n</m>.
                    Make a conjecture about which natural numbers <m>n</m> have
                    <m>n</m>-factors.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <title>Option 7: Traversable Graphs</title>

                <p>
                    A graph is <term>traversable</term> if you can travel along
                    each edge exactly once, and end where you started.
                    Which of the following graphs are traversable?
                    Give an example of an additional traversable graph, and an
                    example of a graph that is not traversable.
                    Make a conjecture about graphs that are or are not
                    traversable.
                    (It doesn't have to completely solve the problem,
                    conjectures about <q>special cases</q> are good too!)
                </p>

                <figure xml:id="fig-graphs2">
                    <caption>Graphs</caption>
                    <image source="images/graphs.png" width="75%"/>
                </figure>
            </li>

            <li><title>Option 8: McNuggets</title> <p/></li>
        </dl>
    </p>
</handout>